1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,320
In this tutorial, we'll cover how to scan a website for vulnerabilities using Burp Scanner,

2
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:19,200
how to monitor what scans are doing while they're running, and how to export results.

3
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:28,560
There are two basic ways of performing vulnerability scans using Burp. First, you can let Burp do an

4
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:36,000
end-to-end managed scan. That means crawling to discover content and functionality, and then

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,600
auditing for vulnerabilities. The second way is that you can select individual items to be audited.

6
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:51,840
Let's look at the first way, which is performing an end-to-end managed scan. The way you launch one

7
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:59,200
of these is you go to the Burp dashboard and you click on New Scan. This opens the scan launcher.

8
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,960
You see at the top, you have an option for the scan type, so you could select whether to do

9
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:09,920
a crawl and audit, which is the default, or whether just to crawl to discover content.

10
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:16,560
So we're going to leave it on Crawl and Audit. Then you need to enter a URL or multiple URLs to scan.

11
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:25,360
So I'm going to enter a URL there. Under Protocol Settings, you have an option whether to scan using

12
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:33,040
both HTTP and HTTPS, which is the default, or whether to scan just using the specified protocols

13
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:37,760
that you've given. In general, if you aren't absolutely sure whether an application uses both

14
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:42,720
protocols and has content and functionality on each one, it's best to leave the default setting.

15
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:50,880
That's all you actually need to do to perform a default scan, is just give up the URL and click OK.

16
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:56,560
But we're just going to walk through some other options that you can use to fine-tune the behavior

17
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:01,920
of the scanner and optimize it for different purposes. So down at the bottom here, we have

18
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:09,440
detailed scope configuration. And here you can configure in more detail exactly what is in and out

19
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:15,680
of the scope. So it will always begin crawling from the start URLs that you've given it. But here you

20
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:23,600
can specify additional URL prefixes that should be included or URL prefixes that should be excluded.

21
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:29,680
So if you have other domains or other folders that you want to include in the scan, you can add them

22
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:34,320
under the included prefixes. If you have parts of an application that you don't want to include,

23
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:40,880
you put them under exclude. The scan configuration section is where you can configure a mass of other

24
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:47,280
options to control how the scanner works. One way to do this is to add a new configuration and you can

25
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:54,080
specify whether it's for crawling or auditing. So let's start with crawling. And here we have sections

26
00:02:54,080 --> 00:03:00,480
for various areas of configuration and you can expand each of these to define it. So crawl optimization,

27
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:07,920
you can control the link depth, you can configure the crawl strategy as to whether you prefer speed

28
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:15,360
versus deep coverage. Under crawl limits, you can set some limits for how long the crawl should take

29
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:23,840
or the maximum number of locations or the maximum number of requests. You can control how the crawler

30
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:30,400
handles application login functions. So by default, burp will attempt to self-register a user if it

31
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:35,440
discovers a user registration function, it will populate that and attempt to use the credentials

32
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:41,760
to log in with during the scan. You can also trigger login failures. This means submitting invalid

33
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:48,000
credentials to reach functionality or behavior that is accessed behind a failed login. So that might be

34
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:53,440
a forgotten password feature. You can control how burp will handle application errors during the crawl.

35
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:58,880
So whether you want to pause or stop the crawl if there are too many timeouts because the application

36
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:03,200
maybe isn't responding properly. And there are some other miscellaneous options as well.

37
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:10,720
So if we create a configuration for auditing, it works in the same way, but we have a bunch of

38
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:18,560
different options. So under audit optimization, you can configure whether the scanner should optimize for

39
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:25,760
a faster scan or a more thorough scan, depending on what kind of payloads are being sent. You can also

40
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:32,640
configure the audit accuracy so you can tell burp whether to favor fewer false negatives or fewer

41
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:38,000
false positives. So there are some scan checks, some types of techniques that burp can use to find

42
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,240
vulnerabilities which are inherently prone to false positives because of the kind of evidence that they

43
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:50,960
gather. So one example of those is a SQL injection payload, which if successful will cause a time delay.

44
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:57,520
Sometimes time delays happen anyway because of varying latency in the application's responses or other

45
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:03,040
delays. And sometimes they could line up with burp's payloads. So burp will repeat those payloads several

46
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:07,840
times to establish a correlation, but this is where you can configure how much of a correlation to look

47
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:15,040
for and whether to favor false positives or false negatives. You can configure the kind of issues that

48
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:21,360
burp will report. So you can select these by the type of scan technique that is being used. So at one

49
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:27,840
extreme we have passive techniques, passive issues, which can be reported just by examining the

50
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:34,560
application's normal traffic, sending normal requests. The other extreme we have intrusive active scans,

51
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:40,800
which involve a lot of more heavy payloads being sent to the application. These can, in some applications,

52
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:46,320
cause damage if the application is vulnerable. And in between you have light and medium active

53
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:52,320
vulnerabilities. Burp scanner also does JavaScript analysis. So it will use static analysis and dynamic

54
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:58,080
analysis techniques on the rendered page on the DOM to discover vulnerabilities and you can configure

55
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:05,440
whether to do that. If you prefer, you can configure individual vulnerabilities to be checked for,

56
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:11,760
and you can configure the specific detection methods that are being used to find those. So as you can

57
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:19,520
see, burp can scan for masses and masses of different vulnerabilities. You can configure how

58
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:24,480
application errors are handled during the audit, similar to during the crawl. So you can skip

59
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:31,280
insertion points or skip audit items if you see too many errors. You can configure the types of insertion

60
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:36,800
points that are used. So you can configure whether that will place payloads into locations like URL

61
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:45,040
parameters, body parameters, and other types. You can optionally tell burp to move some parameters to

62
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:49,360
different locations and audit them again there. So this occasionally is useful. You will have some

63
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:54,800
applications where there's a parameter that is normally in the URL and there's something like a web

64
00:06:54,800 --> 00:07:00,320
application firewall that is filtering payloads that appear in the URL. But if you switch that parameter to

65
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:05,040
the body, it still reaches the application. The application is agnostic as to where it appears,

66
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:10,720
but it bypasses the WAF and you can maybe reach a vulnerability. This ends up taking a fair bit

67
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:15,840
longer in the scan because we have to do all the work multiple times for different parameters,

68
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:22,960
but sometimes that's really useful. You can configure whether to ignore certain types of insertion points.

69
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:28,880
There are some well-known insertion points based on their name or their type that are part of platform

70
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:33,200
frameworks and features that we don't normally expect to see vulnerabilities in. You can configure

71
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:39,120
those there. You can configure how burp handles frequently occurring insertion points. They're

72
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:44,960
insertion points like cookies where the same parameter will appear everywhere in every request,

73
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:50,880
and burp can do a full audit of that every time it appears, but it's generally not very efficient to do

74
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:57,440
that. By default, what BERT will do for all kinds of insertion points is if it sees them occurring

75
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:04,800
frequently, it will begin doing a normal thorough audit, and if they prove to be boring, so if the

76
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:10,880
same insertion point always leads to the same vulnerabilities or no vulnerabilities at all, it will

77
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:17,520
gradually scale back to a faster scan to be more efficient on those insertion points. There are some

78
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:22,560
other options for insertion points, whether to use nested insertion points to see one type of data

79
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:28,320
inside another, and you can configure some details about how burp does JavaScript analysis, whether to

80
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:33,840
use dynamic techniques or static techniques, and whether to request missing JavaScript dependencies that

81
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:41,520
are needed to render the page. One thing that's worth pointing out about how the scan configuration

82
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:49,040
options work is that within each of these dialogues, each section is collapsed by default, and that means

83
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:55,360
it's not defined, and if you expand it, then you're defining just the options for that section. So if you save

84
00:08:55,360 --> 00:09:01,760
a configuration with one section expanded, you're just defining the settings for that section, and what

85
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:08,640
that means is you can add multiple scan configurations that just configure settings in specific areas, so you might

86
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:13,840
start with your generic general base configuration that you like to use, and then you can add in

87
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:20,160
additional configurations after that to fine-tune different areas of the options. Burp also has a

88
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:26,000
configuration library. This contains various built-in, predefined configurations that you can use for

89
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:31,920
different purposes, and you can also easily create your own custom configurations, save them to the library,

90
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:38,240
and then quickly select them later. Under application login, you can provide Burp with credentials for

91
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:44,000
known accounts that exist, which will be used during the crawl and audit. So if you provide these, when

92
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:50,480
Burp finds a login form, it will log in using those credentials, and if any new content or functionality is

93
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:55,760
discovered, that will be crawled and audited within the user context. So you can add multiple of these, you

94
00:09:55,760 --> 00:10:02,160
might have one for ordinary users, or you might have one for a manager role within an application or an

95
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:08,560
administrator, and Burp will use each of these separately and discover content. Finally, under resource pools,

96
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:15,280
you can configure how Burp will make use of your network resources that you have. So you can

97
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:20,880
configure the maximum number of concurrent requests that the scan can make, or you can configure the

98
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:28,000
delay between requests if you want to impose some throttling. This can be useful if you have a very

99
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:35,200
fragile application that you can't scan very quickly, you can make Burp slow down, or if you are doing a lot of

100
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:41,440
scans in parallel, and you don't want to overwhelm your own machine or your network connection, you can

101
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:47,760
limit the total amount of traffic that can be generated from all the scans together. Anyway,

102
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:53,440
to launch a scan, all you really need is the URL. So that's what we're going to provide, and then we're

103
00:10:53,440 --> 00:11:01,120
going to click OK to launch the scan. So when we do that, you can see in the dashboard, in the task list,

104
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:07,600
we have this new entry which represents the scan, and it says, call an audit of the URL that I provided.

105
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:14,240
You can see in this status line here, the scan progresses through various different phases,

106
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:19,040
and it tells you what you're doing, and it gives a progress bar for each phase, so you can see how

107
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:24,960
the scan is progressing. What you also have is the request count, the numbers of errors that have been

108
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:30,400
encountered, and the number of locations that have been discovered during the crawl. And then when the scan

109
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:35,600
moves on to the audit phase, you can see the number of issues that have been discovered. What you also

110
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:42,240
have on the top right is the issue activity log, and this is where vulnerabilities will appear in real

111
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:48,480
time as they're discovered for you to monitor the progress of the scan. You also have an event log

112
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:54,800
where various information, various errors, any other details that you might need to be aware of will appear.

113
00:11:54,800 --> 00:12:02,320
Now as well as the issue activity log, the other main place where issues get reported is in the target tab.

114
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:08,880
So this will give you a site map of all the content that has been discovered. It will let you see all of

115
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:14,960
the individual requested responses that were found during the crawl, and it will also report all of the

116
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:22,960
vulnerabilities that were found. And you can drill in to individual folders and see the vulnerabilities

117
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:29,200
that exist just within those paths, or you can look at everything together. The way that reports issues is

118
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:34,880
that it gives you all of the information that you need, all of the evidence to understand the issue. So

119
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:39,680
here we have a cross-site scripting vulnerability, and we can see the full request and the highlighted

120
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:45,360
payload that was sent. And then in the response, we can see the payload came back and the site is

121
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:51,600
vulnerable. Different types of vulnerability will report different evidence. So here we have one that

122
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:58,400
is using the collaborator to detect an external service interaction. So we send a payload with a URL

123
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:03,120
on the collaborator, and that caused a DNS interaction for that domain name.

124
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:12,720
The second way of performing vulnerability scans is to send individual items to be audited. So anywhere

125
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:18,560
in Burp, you can select one or more requests, choose scan from the context menu and send them to scanning.

126
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:27,760
So I'm going to go to Burp proxy and open Burps browser, and I'm going to visit a URL.

127
00:13:27,760 --> 00:13:35,520
So I'm going to forward all the traffic through the proxy. And then in the history, we can see

128
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:40,320
the request that happened. I could select one of these and do scan.

129
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:48,560
If we do that, the scan launcher opens in the normal way, but we now have a third option, which is to audit

130
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:55,120
the items that I had selected. And here we can see that one URL that I'd selected is in the list.

131
00:13:55,120 --> 00:14:00,640
All the other options are the same, except for application login, because we're not going to

132
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:06,400
do a crawl now. There are no login settings. And to audit that single item, we just click OK,

133
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:15,600
and the audit begins. If we go to the dashboard, we can see we have this new task, just doing an audit

134
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:26,640
of that single item. If we do that again, we select a different item and do scan. We now select open scan

135
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:35,200
launcher. Scan launcher opens for this different item. The first time we launched a scan in this way,

136
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:44,160
Burp created a new scan task to do the work. We now have the option whether to add this new item to that

137
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:50,880
same task or whether to create a new task for it. And what that means we can do is we can select different

138
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:57,440
scan configurations to use for different items. I'm going to go to the configuration library and select

139
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:08,160
Light Active Audit Checks. If I launch this scan and go to the dashboard, you can now see there are two audit tasks.

140
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:14,880
The first one using the default configuration and the second one using the audit checks Light Active

141
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:22,160
configuration. Now having done that, what we can do if we want is to select a different item. And when we

142
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:30,560
look at the scan context menu, we can see we can open the scan launcher or we can send this item directly

143
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:36,880
to one of our existing tasks. So this is a really nice way to have different tasks set up and running

144
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:44,560
with different configurations. And as you work manually, you can direct individual requests to a specific task

145
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:52,480
with a specific configuration. That's all the ways of launching scans. So now let's take a look at how you can

146
00:15:52,480 --> 00:16:01,760
monitor scans as they're progressing and view the results. So on the dashboard entry for a task, you can click on

147
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:21,440
view details or this little icon to open some more details about the scan in progress. So on this details tab, we have pretty much the same details that are shown on the face of the dashboard. You can see requests, errors, locations that were crawled, numbers of issues discovered during the audit.

148
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:27,760
You can also open the configuration for the scan and change some of the settings as it's in progress.

149
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:36,720
You go to the audit items tab. This shows you all of the items that have been discovered during the crawl,

150
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:42,320
and you can view their progress through the different phases of the audit. So you can see the passive phases,

151
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:51,280
the active phases, the JavaScript work. You can see the full URL. You can see the number of issues that have been reported for each item, numbers of requests and errors.

152
00:16:51,680 --> 00:17:03,200
If you double click on an item, you can see the base request and response. So this really gives you complete visibility into what BIRP is doing during the scan.

153
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:19,760
You have the issue activity tab. This contains the same detail as on the dashboard, but just for this one task, this one scan. So you can select individual issues and you can drill into the evidence to examine them as before.

154
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:24,880
And you also have the event log, which just contains the event log entries for this task.

155
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:32,480
Finally, if you want to generate a scan report containing the issues that were discovered,

156
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:42,160
you can do that. Anywhere that you see issues, you can select one or select many and choose report selected issues from the context menu.

157
00:17:42,160 --> 00:17:49,440
You can also in the sitemap, select a folder or a host and go to issues and say report issues for this host.

158
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:55,360
So if we do that, we open the reporting wizard, we can choose HTML or XML format.

159
00:17:56,720 --> 00:18:00,000
You can select which types of details to include in the report.

160
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:09,600
You can select whether to include full HTTP requests and responses or just relevant extracts to avoid the report getting too big.

161
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:14,640
You can select which issue types to include if you want to exclude some of them.

162
00:18:15,680 --> 00:18:17,200
And then you can save the report.

163
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:24,160
So if we give the report a name and save it.

164
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:27,920
That report has been generated.

165
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:30,800
We can then open that report and view it.

166
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:33,360
Here we can see the report.

167
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:38,480
We have a summary at the top showing us the number of issues that have been reported by severity and confidence.

168
00:18:39,200 --> 00:18:45,840
And we can drill down and see all of the individual issues and each one by default will include all of the

169
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:48,560
background remediation detail.

170
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:54,400
And where applicable, it will have requests and responses, including the highlights of the interesting parts.

171
00:18:55,760 --> 00:19:00,400
So that's how to scan a website for vulnerabilities using Burp Scanner.

172
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:17,440
So that's how to monitor what scans are doing while they're running and how to report results.

